### What is the Observer pattern?

Observer defines a one-to-many dependency: when the subject changes, all registered observers are notified. The subject exposes `subscribe` and `unsubscribe` methods and holds a list of observers.

```java
package com.bank.observer;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.function.Consumer;

public class TransferPublisher {
    private final List<Consumer<TransferEvent>> listeners = new ArrayList<>();

    public void subscribe(Consumer<TransferEvent> listener) {
        listeners.add(listener);
    }

    public void publish(TransferEvent event) {
        listeners.forEach(l -> l.accept(event));
    }
}

public record TransferEvent(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {}
```

```java
publisher.subscribe(e -> System.out.println("AUDIT: " + e));
publisher.subscribe(e -> System.out.println("SMS to Priya: Transfer of INR " + e.amount()));
publisher.publish(new TransferEvent("SAV2001", "SAV3002", new BigDecimal("50000")));
```

```text
AUDIT: TransferEvent[fromId=SAV2001, toId=SAV3002, amount=50000]
SMS to Priya: Transfer of INR 50000
```

### What is the Command pattern?

Command encapsulates a request as an object with an `execute` method and often an `undo`. The invoker triggers commands without knowing what they do.

```java
package com.bank.command;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface Command {
    void execute();
    void undo();
}

public class DebitCommand implements Command {
    private final Account account;
    private final BigDecimal amount;

    public DebitCommand(Account account, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.account = account;
        this.amount = amount;
    }

    @Override
    public void execute() { account.debit(amount); }

    @Override
    public void undo() { account.credit(amount); }
}
```

```java
Command cmd = new DebitCommand(rajeshAccount, new BigDecimal("10000"));
cmd.execute(); // debits 10000
cmd.undo();    // credits 10000 back
```

### What is the Iterator pattern?

Iterator provides sequential access to a collection without exposing its structure. Every Java collection returns one.

```java
package com.bank.iterator;

import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.List;

public class TradeIteratorDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> trades = List.of("BUY RELIANCE", "SELL HDFC", "BUY ICICI");
        Iterator<String> it = trades.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            System.out.println("Processing: " + it.next());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Processing: BUY RELIANCE
Processing: SELL HDFC
Processing: BUY ICICI
```

### What is the State pattern?

State lets an object alter its behaviour when its internal state changes. The object delegates to a state object, and each state class implements the behaviour differently.

```java
package com.bank.state;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface AccountState {
    void withdraw(AccountContext ctx, BigDecimal amount);
}

public class ActiveState implements AccountState {
    @Override
    public void withdraw(AccountContext ctx, BigDecimal amount) {
        ctx.setBalance(ctx.getBalance().subtract(amount));
        System.out.println("Withdrawn INR " + amount);
    }
}

public class FrozenState implements AccountState {
    @Override
    public void withdraw(AccountContext ctx, BigDecimal amount) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("Account is frozen, cannot withdraw");
    }
}
```

```java
AccountContext account = new AccountContext(new BigDecimal("100000"));
account.setState(new ActiveState());
account.withdraw(new BigDecimal("5000"));  // works
account.setState(new FrozenState());
account.withdraw(new BigDecimal("5000"));  // throws IllegalStateException
```

### What is the Template Method pattern?

Template Method defines the invariant steps of an algorithm in a base method and defers variable steps to subclasses.

```java
package com.bank.template;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public abstract class SettlementTemplate {
    // Template method: fixed skeleton
    public final void settle(String tradeId, BigDecimal amount) {
        validate(tradeId);
        execute(tradeId, amount);  // varies by instrument
        record(tradeId, amount);
    }

    private void validate(String tradeId) {
        System.out.println("Validating trade: " + tradeId);
    }

    protected abstract void execute(String tradeId, BigDecimal amount);

    private void record(String tradeId, BigDecimal amount) {
        System.out.println("Recording settlement of INR " + amount);
    }
}

public class EquitySettlement extends SettlementTemplate {
    @Override
    protected void execute(String tradeId, BigDecimal amount) {
        System.out.println("Settling equity trade " + tradeId
                         + " for INR " + amount);
    }
}
```

```text
Validating trade: TRD001
Settling equity trade TRD001 for INR 250000
Recording settlement of INR 250000
```

### What is the Visitor pattern?

Visitor adds a new operation over a set of element classes without modifying them. Each element accepts a visitor that performs the operation.

```java
package com.bank.visitor;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface AccountVisitor {
    void visit(SavingsAccount sa);
    void visit(CurrentAccount ca);
}

public class InterestReportVisitor implements AccountVisitor {
    @Override
    public void visit(SavingsAccount sa) {
        BigDecimal interest = sa.getBalance()
            .multiply(new BigDecimal("0.04"));
        System.out.println("Savings interest: INR " + interest);
    }

    @Override
    public void visit(CurrentAccount ca) {
        System.out.println("Current accounts earn no interest");
    }
}
```

```java
SavingsAccount sa = new SavingsAccount(new BigDecimal("500000"));
sa.accept(new InterestReportVisitor());
```

```text
Savings interest: INR 20000
```

### What is the Mediator pattern?

Mediator centralises communication between many objects so they do not reference each other directly.

```java
package com.bank.mediator;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class TradingDeskMediator {
    private final List<Trader> traders = new ArrayList<>();

    public void register(Trader trader) { traders.add(trader); }

    public void broadcast(String message, Trader sender) {
        traders.stream()
            .filter(t -> t != sender)
            .forEach(t -> t.receive(message));
    }
}
```

### What is the Memento pattern?

Memento captures and restores an object's state without exposing its internals.

```java
package com.bank.memento;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AccountMemento {
    private final BigDecimal balance;
    private final String status;
    AccountMemento(BigDecimal balance, String status) {
        this.balance = balance; this.status = status;
    }
    BigDecimal getBalance() { return balance; }
    String getStatus() { return status; }
}

public class BankAccount {
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private String status;

    public AccountMemento save() {
        return new AccountMemento(balance, status);
    }

    public void restore(AccountMemento m) {
        this.balance = m.getBalance();
        this.status = m.getStatus();
    }
}
```

```java
AccountMemento snapshot = account.save();
account.withdraw(new BigDecimal("50000"));
// Something went wrong, roll back
account.restore(snapshot);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

In modern Java several of these patterns shrink to lambdas. Command is often just a `Runnable`. Observer listeners are lambdas. Iterator is hidden inside the for-each loop and streams. The pattern intent still guides the design even when the actual Java form is a single line.

```java
// Command as Runnable
Runnable cmd = () -> account.debit(new BigDecimal("10000"));
cmd.run();

// Observer as lambda
publisher.subscribe(e -> log.info("Transfer: " + e));

// Iterator hidden in streams
trades.stream().forEach(t -> process(t));
```

Recognising both the intent and the lightweight form is what shows these patterns are applied the way they really appear in production.
